Embedding Functional Biomacromolecules within PeptidesDirected Metal-Organic Frameworks Nanoarchitectures Enables Activity Enhancement
## Introduction
Biomacromolecules are a class of natural assemblies that can control a series of biotransformation in living cell, with much higher efficiency, specificity and selectivity compared with artificial biosystem.[1.2] Adopting biomacromolecules, for example enzymes, for cell-free biotransformation usually require the immobilization of them within poroussupports,becauseoftheinherentfragilityof biomacromolecules.[3-5] Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of porous materials assembled by metal nodes and organic ligands, with ultrahigh surface area, tailorable porosity, and versatile framework compositions.[6-9] These unique merits endow MOFs have huge potentials for hosting guest compared with traditional materials.[9-11] Very recently,  the scientists  found  that biomacromolecules enabled to be entrapped in-situ into the growing MOFs via de novoapproaches such as coprecipitation and biomineralization.[12-19] The symbiotic biomacromolecules@MOFs biohybrids have appealing advantages that allows to mimic cell activity.[20] For example, the ultrahigh porosity of MOFs provides cytomembrane-like structure for hosting biomolecules, and the opening MOFs networks allows the selective transport of the substrate that mimics the biotransformation process in living cell.
Up till now, 3D zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs, ${\sim}3.5$ A aperture) are the most widely explored MOFs for hosting bioentitiesl12-20], owing to their biocompatible synthesis conditions that shielding biomacromolecules against denaturation during the MOFs biohybrids formation. The activity of the biomacromolecule after entrappment by a 3D microporous ZlFs, however,is still far from satisfactory. For instance, the biomacromolecules (horseradish peroxidase, lipase, catalase, etc.) confined within 3D ZIF-8 or ZIF-90 usually retain extremely low bioactivity $(<10\%$ of its original activity) or even complete inactivity[14,21.2] . To improve the biofunctionality of MOFs biohybrids, it is urgent need to understand the role of MOFs structure on the activity of the hosted biomacromolecules, and seek an alternative strategy that facilitates to construct MOF biohybrids preserving high activity. Unfortunately, it is still challenge to study this structure-activity relationship， owing to the susceptibility of fragile biomacromolecules  that  embarrasses toengineerthe nanoarchitectures of MOFs hybrids.
Herein, we report a practical and biocompatible strategy that enables to tailor the ZiF-8- shrouded biohybrids into controllable nanoarchitectures in-situ. The formed ZlF-8 coating is feasible to be shaped from different 3D microporous architectures to 2D mesoporous layer by a peptide modulator, y-poly-L-glutamic acid (PLGA). PLGA, a biocompatible anionic polypeptide with abundant carboxylic groups (Figure S1),is used as a biomacromolecules surface modifier by electrostatic interaction， which significantly facilitates the MOFs formation around the biomacromolecules. Meanwhile, the excess PLGA chain can competitively coordinate with metal ions by carboxylic groups, resulting in the morphologic evolution and generating defect structures in the composites (Scheme 1). Based on this mild strategy,we enable to comprehensively evaluate the effect of the MOFs nanostructure on the biomacromolecules' activity. We demonstrate biomacromolecules (e,g, enzymes) entrapped within the novel 2D mesoporous spindle-shaped MOFs architectures (2D MSMOFs) can significantly boost the activity of
MOFs biohybrids, because the shortened diffusion path and enlarged pore channel in 2D MSMOFs facilitates the intercommunication. Furthermore, the engineered 2D MSMOFs thin layer also shields the hosted biomacromolecules as well as their biominerals against unfolding, and thus allows them to perform their biological functions in uninhabitable environments.
## Results and Discussion
Peptides-directed morphologic evolution of MOFs biohybrids.
Horseradish peroxidase (HRP), an iron-heme enzyme that catalyzes the oxidization of substrates with hydrogen peroxide, was firstly adopted as the biomacromolecule model. Our previous study found that high concentration of 2-methyl imidazole (HmlM, the ligand of ZIF-8) gave risk to inactivate the enzymes.[23] The relatively low concentration of HmlM ( $480~\mathsf{m M}$ in the reaction system) was chosen, because the activity of enzymes can be well maintained under this condition (Figure S2). In the typical de novo encapsulation, HRP was dispersed into the peptides water solution, following by adding metal ion (zinc acetate) and HmlM. The mixture was aged and the biomacromolecules@MOFs biohybrids was then formed (Scheme 1). Previous researches have revealed that the negative charges, contributed by the carboxylic groups of amino acid residues of biomacromolecule, play a key role on accelerating the MOFs formation around the biomacromolecules[12.17,24] HRP with positive surface charges was unable to induce the MOFs formation (Figure S3A). While PLGA was introduced, the PLGA-modified HRP (by electrostatic interaction) with abundant carboxylic groups facilitated the formation of biomacromolecules $@$ MOFs composite (Figure S3B) This rapid self-induced MOFs formation enabled to reduce the risk of inactivation of biomacromolecule during the encapsulation process.[23] The morphological evolution of the obtained composites were studied by scanning electron microscope (SEM, Figure 1A and Figure S4A) and transmission electron microscope (TEM, Figure S4D), respectively. When the encapsulation undergone without PLGA modulator, the composites grew into a uniform 3D rhombohedron dodecahedron, exhibiting diameter of ca. $1.5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure S4B).With the addition of PLGA from 1 to $5\mathsf{m g}$ , the biohybrids gradually evolved into an irregular 3D polyhedron with folded surface. Increasing the PLGA dosage leaded to the morphological evolution to a 3D decussation $(10\mathsf{m g})$ and finally to a 2D spindle-shaped layer $(20\mathsf{m}\mathsf{g})$ with ca. $150\mathsf{n m}$ thickness (Figure S4C). Further improving the PLGA dosage resulted in the assembly of 2D layer into a 3D flower-like architecture. The corresponding evolution of Bragg diffraction peaks also supported that PLGA enabled to regulate the crystal growth of the biomacromolecules@MOFs biohybrids,where the typical 3D ZIF8 diffraction peaks became weak, accompanying by the occurrence of the new diffraction peaks ascribed to the 2D layer structure of ZIF$8^{[25]}$ (Figure 1B). A
We next sought to confirm that such morphological evolution was indeed attributed by the competitive coordination effect of peptide. The incorporation of PLGA was firstly verified by Fourier transform infrared spectrum (FTiR) test. The spectral component centered at ca. $1030\ c m^{-1}$ ,assigned to the N-H bending vibration of amide in PLGA, gradually appeared in the biomacromolecules@MOFs biohybrids when PLGA modulator was increased (Figure S5A). In addition, the new absorption band centered at ca. $1569\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ ，which was assigned to the asymmetric stretching vibration of the coordinated carboxyl[26], was also recorded in the MOFs biohybrids, but not be discovered in free PLGA (Figure S5B). It gave the first evidence that the incorporated PLGA coordinated with the metal ions. The MOFs biohybrids were then collected for X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) test, and the elements of Zn, C, N and O were successfully recorded (Figure S6). High resolution of Zn 2p3 spectra showed that the binding energy was $1021.4~\mathsf{e V}$ for the composites without PLGA, while it gradually shifted to a higher region of 1021.7 eV for the assembled composites with PLGA (Figure 2A). These results together indicated the new Zn-O bond was formed through the competitive coordination of PLGA with $Z n^{2+}$ (Figure 2B).
The competitive coordination by modulator usually enables to create the defects in ${\mathsf{M O F s}}^{[27]}$ ，we hypothesize that the coordinated PLGA herein could create the defects that enables to tailor the mesopores in the MOFs biohybrids. The nitrogen adsorption isotherms were carried out to verify our assumptions (Figure 2C). The calculated Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas of the MOFs biohybrids gradually decreased from $1777.3\ \mathsf{c m}^{2}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ to $96.3~{\mathsf{c m}}^{2}~{\mathsf{g}}^{-1}$ when the PLGA dosage increased (Table S1), causing by the incorporation of PLGA and biomacromolecule in the composites.Pore size distribution analysis revealed that only micropores of $1.1\ \mathsf{n m}$ on average can be observed in the biomacromolecules $@3\mathsf{D}$ MOFs biohybrids without PLGA (Figure S7A). As anticipated, the micropores translated into mesopores ranging from $2\mathsf{n m}$ to $20\mathsf{n m}$ in the 2D spindle-shaped layer and 3D follower-like biohybrids (Figure 2D and Figure S7B). These results together verified that the PGLA peptide modulator enabled to modulate the nanoarchitecture and aperture size of the MOFs biohybrids.
We next confirmed the MOFs-encapsulated biomacromolecules structure was formed. The loading contents of the proteins within the composites was measured by examining the concentration differences of proteins in the supernatant before and after encapsulation via Bradford proteins assay (Table S2).[28] To verify that the proteins were indeed encapsulated within, instead of being adsorbed onto the MOFs, the MOFs biohybrids were analyzed by
