## The effect of the MOFs nanostursture on the activity
The comparable proteins loading concents (Table S2) between different MOFs biohybrids indicated the pepetids-directed strategy has negligible impact on the encapsulation. We then evaluated the effect of the MOFs nanostursture on the activity of the formed biohybrids.The visual catalytic process of the encapsulated HRP was recorded using 3,3',5,5'-tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) as a peroxidase substrate. As shown in Figure 3A, the HRP@3D microporous MOFs displayed extremely low activity because of the hindered diffusion of the substrates in this 3D microporous architecture. Such low activity was also observed in the previous reported where enzymes was embedded in 3D microporous MOFs.[21,23] As a comparison, the
HRP@2D MSMOFs was capable of catalyzing TMB into a blue color product (oxTMB)instantaneously. To comprehensively evaluate the effect of theMOFs nanostructure onthe encapsulated biomacromolecules' activity,the catalytic kinetic curves of different HRP@MOFs biohybrids were monitored by UV-vis spectroscopy (Figure 3B). The relative enzymatic activity, denoted as the catalytic rate herein were calculated as follow: HRP@2D MSMOFs $\gg$ HRP $@30$ decussation MOFs $\approx\mathsf{H R P}@3\mathsf{D}$ nanoflower MOFs $>$ $\mathsf{H R P}@3\mathsf{D}$ polyhedron MOFs (Figure 3C). It showed that the preserved bioacticvity was related to the nanostrusture of the MOFs hybrids, wherein the 2D architecture and mesoporous aperture favored the high activity preservation. Specifically， enzyme $@20$ MSMOFs exhibited unprecedented activity, which was compable to the free counterparts (Figure 3B). In contrast, the most widely explored enzyme@3D microporous ZIF-8 without PLGA modulator retained only $0.6\%$ of activity. This is consistent with previous researches, where microporous ZiF-encapsulated enzymes maintained less than $10\%$ of activity compared with free counterparts.[14,21,22,29]
To further evaluate the catalytic efficiency of HRP@MOFs biohybrids, the catalytic kinetic process were monitored. Figure S11 depicted the time-dependent formation of catalytic products by HRP $@20$ MSMOFs and $\mathsf{H R P}@3\mathsf{D}$ microporous MOFs, respectively, and calculated the Michaelis-Menten kinetic parameters was showed in Figure S12. The catalytic efficiency (denotes as $k_{c a t}/K_{m}$ ，where $k_{c a t}$ and $\textstyle{K_{m}}$ are the catalyst rate constant and Michaelis-Menten constant, respectively) was $356\mathsf{m}\mathsf{M}^{-1}\mathsf{S}^{-1}$ for HRP $@20$ MSMOFs, while only 9.8 mM-1 $\mathsf{S}^{-1}$ for $\mathsf{H R P}@3\mathsf{D}$ microporous MOFs. In addition,HRP $@20$ MSMOFs exhibited a much higher catalytic power (high $k_{c a t}$ and $V_{m a x}$ values,where $V_{m a x}$ is the maximum reaction rate) than the reported peroxidase-mimicking nanozymes (Table S3).[30-32] Such remarkably elevation of bioactivity corresponded to our anticipation that shaping the MOFs coating into 2D layer with mesoporous structure enabled to shorten the diffusion path of substrate and enhanced the accessibility of hosted biomacromolecule, thus boosting the activity of MOFs biohybrids.
The universality of the engineered 2D mesoporous MOFs architecture for improving the activity of biohybrids was ascertained by embedding other proteins including cytochrome C (Cyt C） and glucose oxidase $(\mathsf{G O x})$ into the 2D MSMOFs. Previous studies revealed that proteins with different surface charges could affect the crystallization of ZIF-8.[23.24,33 Thus, we firstly confirmed that proteins with different surface charges (Cyt C has positive surface charge while GOx has negative surface charge) enabled to be encapsulated within a 2D MSMOFs using our approach (Figure S13). The morphological evolution, evaluated by SEM and PXRD,indicated that the biohybrids with 2D spindle-shaped layer architecture were formed through modulating the PLGA dosage (Figure S14A-S14D). In addition, the obtained MOFs biohybrids possessed the apparent colour of the corresponding parent proteins (Figure S15A), and CLSM images revealed that both of Cyt C and GOx were uniformly embedded within the 2D MOFs, respectively (Figure S15B, S15C). Catalytic kinetic monitoring (Figure S16) indicated that 4.3- and 11.6- fold enhancements in bioactivity were observed in Cyt $\mathtt{C@}$ and ${\mathsf{G O x@20}}$ MSMOFs, compared with the corresponding proteins $@3\mathsf{D}$ microporous MOFs (Figure 3D), ascertaining the universality of our approach for constructing 2 D MSMOFs biohybrids that perserves activity.
